course: dictionary
lesson: 1090
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word bracket has more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. In maths, brackets are the marks ( ) that show which part of a sum to work out first. In writing, they hold extra information.
explain: «Work out what is in brackets first.» Here bracket means the round marks, not a shelf support. The words around it, like sum and work out, point to maths. A shelf or a wall would point to the support meaning.
explain: «The 8–10 age bracket» is a group or range, the ages from 8 to 10. Same word, different job. Ask yourself: what would fit here? If you swap in shelf support, the sentence stops making sense, so that is not the meaning.
fact: Brackets are the marks ( ) put around numbers in a sum to show which part to work out first.
fact: «Code follows the same order as maths, times before plus, so 2 + 3 * 4 is 14, and brackets go first, so (2 + 3) * 4 is 20.»
fact: In writing, brackets hold extra words that are not needed for the sentence to make sense, like «My dog (a spaniel) loves walks».
fact: Brackets come in kinds: round ( ), square [ ] and curly { }. In the USA, round brackets are called parentheses.
fact: A bracket can also be a metal or wooden support fixed to a wall to hold up a shelf, or a group or range, like «the 8–10 age bracket».
exercise: «Work out what is in brackets first.» In a sum, what do brackets tell you to do? | Add it to the end | Write it in capital letters | *Work out that part first | Ignore that part completely
exercise: Use the rule: «(2 + 3) * 4». What is the answer? | 24 | 11 | 14 | *20
exercise: Dad fixed a metal bracket to the wall to hold up a shelf. What is a bracket here? | A round mark in a sum | A group of ages | *A support that holds up a shelf | A kind of dash
test: «The 8–10 age bracket» means what? | A shelf support on a wall | A sum with round marks | A short, rude reply | *A group or range, such as the ages 8 to 10
test: In «My dog (a spaniel) loves walks», what do the brackets hold? | A support for a shelf | *Extra information that the sentence does not need to make sense | A group of dogs | A mark in a sum
exam: Which sentence uses brackets for extra information? | «(2 + 3) * 4 is 20.» | «The 8–10 age bracket is wide.» | *«My dog (a spaniel) loves walks.» | «A metal bracket holds up the shelf.»
